1
00:00:00,875 --> 00:00:13,012
Namo tassa bhagavato harahato sambha sambuddhasa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

2
00:00:15,855 --> 00:00:22,344
So I thought we'd spend at least an occasional meeting on the scriptures themselves.

3
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:25,649
And let me just do this, just like that.

4
00:00:25,951 --> 00:00:28,172
on the scriptures themselves.

5
00:00:28,193 --> 00:00:44,609
And I thought to start with the way the Buddha constantly uses simile, the discourses are just full of them, cleverly used to make us understand things from a slightly more experiential point of view, you might say.

6
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:50,676
So, oh, by the way, I only learned today what a blue moon was.

7
00:00:51,600 --> 00:01:01,000
I knew the phrase once in a blue moon, meaning it's virtually impossible, but I didn't realize it actually meant when you get a second moon in a solar month.

8
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:05,569
And it seems to have arisen from a white American folklore anyway.

9
00:01:06,591 --> 00:01:09,778
So that aside, yeah.

10
00:01:10,112 --> 00:01:25,201
Those of you who've read this masterwork by Ian McGilchrist, The Master and the Emissary, about left brain and right brain, will know that the left brain is associated with logical, analytical, linear, verbal, factual and sequential thinking.

11
00:01:25,772 --> 00:01:35,683
and that the right brain is linked with creative, intuitive, artistic, non-verbal, emotional and imaginative abilities.

12
00:01:36,024 --> 00:01:42,290
Now, actually, according to McGilchrist, these two sides work very well together.

13
00:01:42,751 --> 00:01:43,933
The one needs the other.

14
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:47,956
But really, the left, the right brain should be in charge.

15
00:01:48,016 --> 00:01:48,837
That's what he's saying.

16
00:01:49,459 --> 00:01:50,379
And in our society,

17
00:01:50,680 --> 00:02:17,176
in our culture it's all right brain stuff very linear etc etc so the buddha intuited that i'm sure he didn't know the psychology in the same way that irma gilchrist knew but he definitely intuited that people understand by way of simile imagery metaphor uh what might be difficult to understand as just a straightforward logical explanation

18
00:02:17,713 --> 00:02:21,377
So we call these similes upaya, skillful means.

19
00:02:25,603 --> 00:02:29,747
So I've just chosen some, and most of them I think you will know.

20
00:02:29,788 --> 00:02:31,569
They're very famous.

21
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:36,355
So first of all, how much patience, forbearance should we have?

22
00:02:37,717 --> 00:02:41,502
So this is talking about right speech.

23
00:02:41,943 --> 00:02:45,747
So there are five aspects of speech by which others may address you.

24
00:02:46,147 --> 00:02:56,562
timely or untimely, true or false, affectionate or harsh, beneficial or unbeneficial, with a mind of goodwill or with a mind of hate.

25
00:02:56,981 --> 00:02:58,985
In any event, you should train yourselves.

26
00:02:59,645 --> 00:03:05,252
Our minds will not be affected and we will say no evil words.

27
00:03:05,413 --> 00:03:11,480
We will remain sympathetic to that person's welfare with a mind of goodwill and with no inner hate.

28
00:03:11,831 --> 00:03:31,492
We will keep pervading them with an awareness imbued with goodwill and beginning with them, we will keep pervading the all-encompassing world with an awareness imbued with goodwill equal to a cat-skin bag, abundant, expansive, immeasurable, free from hostility, free from ill will.

29
00:03:31,992 --> 00:03:33,634
That's how you should train yourself.

30
00:03:34,915 --> 00:03:38,699
A cat-skin bag, I presume it's made from cat skins.

31
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:50,461
But so he's saying that we should develop this affectionate awareness no matter what, even when somebody is very rude, very harsh, saying silly things to us, etc.,

32
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:53,325
But now he reinforces it.

33
00:03:53,346 --> 00:03:55,049
How patient should we be?

34
00:03:55,430 --> 00:03:56,912
How forbearing should we be?

35
00:03:56,991 --> 00:03:58,756
So this is a very famous simile.

36
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:15,183
Monks, even if bandits were to carve you up savagely, limb by limb with a two-handle saw, he among you who let his heart get angered, even at that would not be doing my bidding, would not be following my teachings.

37
00:04:15,973 --> 00:04:26,747
Monks, if you attend constantly to this admonition on the simile of the saw, do you see any aspects of speech, slight or gross, that you could not endure?

38
00:04:27,269 --> 00:04:27,810
No, Lord.

39
00:04:28,389 --> 00:04:32,334
Then attend constantly to this admonition on the simile of the saw.

40
00:04:32,776 --> 00:04:36,040
That will be for your long-term welfare and happiness.

41
00:04:36,341 --> 00:04:42,108
This is what the Blessed One said and gratified the monks delighted in the Blessed One's words.

42
00:04:42,509 --> 00:04:43,449
So there we have it.

43
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:50,697
I once read this out in a talk and somebody said, well, the Buddha had a great sense of humor.

44
00:04:51,557 --> 00:04:56,882
But I think he's actually telling us that there's no end to patience.

45
00:04:56,903 --> 00:04:57,302
That's it.

46
00:04:57,444 --> 00:04:59,706
And that anger has no place anywhere.

47
00:05:02,569 --> 00:05:07,574
So then there's a famous one about, you know, the end of training or what's the training for?

48
00:05:08,033 --> 00:05:09,875
And here we have the simile of the rat.

49
00:05:10,598 --> 00:05:16,107
And so he puts this training in perspective to the goal, which is, of course, liberation.

50
00:05:16,987 --> 00:05:28,305
Supposing there was a person traveling along the road, they'd see a large deluge whose near shore was dubious and perilous, while the far shore was a sanctuary free of peril.

51
00:05:29,107 --> 00:05:32,773
But there was no ferry boat or bridge for crossing over.

52
00:05:32,793 --> 00:05:37,500
They'd think, why don't I gather grass, sticks, branches and leaves and make a raft?

53
00:05:38,257 --> 00:05:43,826
Riding on the raft and paddling with my hands and feet, I will safely reach the far shore.

54
00:05:43,846 --> 00:05:46,129
And so they would do exactly that.

55
00:05:46,651 --> 00:05:51,778
And when they'd crossed over to the far shore, they'd think, this raft has been very helpful to me.

56
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:57,608
Riding on the raft and paddling with my hands and feet, I have safely crossed over to the other shore.

57
00:05:58,168 --> 00:06:03,757
Why don't I hoist it on my head or pick it up on my shoulder and go wherever I want?

58
00:06:04,125 --> 00:06:05,466
What do you think, Vendicants?

59
00:06:05,827 --> 00:06:08,870
Would that person be doing what should be done with that raft?

60
00:06:09,351 --> 00:06:09,891
No, sir.

61
00:06:10,593 --> 00:06:13,475
And what, Vendicants, should that person do with a raft?

62
00:06:13,995 --> 00:06:18,180
When they've crossed over, they should think, this raft has been very helpful to me.

63
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:24,086
Why don't I beach it on the dry land or set it adrift in the water and go wherever I want?

64
00:06:24,187 --> 00:06:26,168
That's what a person should do with a raft.

65
00:06:27,029 --> 00:06:31,634
In the same way, I have taught how the teaching is similar to a raft.

66
00:06:32,137 --> 00:06:34,620
It's for crossing over, not for holding on to.

67
00:06:35,281 --> 00:06:43,413
By understanding the simile of the raft, you will even give up the teachings, let alone what is against the teachings.

68
00:06:43,434 --> 00:06:46,076
So I think we have to be slightly careful here.

69
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:57,954
What he's saying is that once we are completely liberated, we don't actually need to remind ourselves about the training because it's becoming viewed in ourselves.

70
00:06:58,915 --> 00:07:01,639
In another discourse,

71
00:07:02,108 --> 00:07:10,555
He's asked, does he actually think about what he's going to say when he's teaching the Dharma or does it come to him spontaneously?

72
00:07:10,815 --> 00:07:12,757
And his reply is again another simile.

73
00:07:12,798 --> 00:07:17,041
He says, well, what about a very highly skilled chariot maker?

74
00:07:17,543 --> 00:07:21,086
Do they think about what they're doing or do they just make the chariot?

75
00:07:21,105 --> 00:07:25,629
Because, you know, they just got that know-how, that physical know-how.

76
00:07:26,211 --> 00:07:27,672
And he says, well, it's like that with me.

77
00:07:28,572 --> 00:07:31,896
Whenever I talk about the Dharma, it comes out spontaneously.

78
00:07:38,761 --> 00:07:40,785
So then we have what is nibbana?

79
00:07:41,185 --> 00:07:47,516
It is simply beyond words, concept images, getting across something that which is inexplicable.

80
00:07:47,997 --> 00:07:48,257
Right.

81
00:07:48,699 --> 00:07:53,908
So here the Buddha uses this famous, you know, this very well-known metaphor of the fire.

82
00:07:54,930 --> 00:08:01,781
So he had, yeah, I think there are three discourses where he's talking to a person called Vachagocha.

83
00:08:02,098 --> 00:08:11,689
And Vachagota is, shall we say, he's very sceptical and he doesn't really get it often.

84
00:08:13,790 --> 00:08:19,057
There was once when he asked the Buddha, look, he says, is there a self or is there not a self?

85
00:08:19,577 --> 00:08:20,879
And the Buddha wouldn't answer.

86
00:08:20,899 --> 00:08:21,920
He was very quiet, you see.

87
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:29,288
And he leaves, you know, with a click in his tongue, you know, like he's fed up.

88
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:32,936
And Ananda asked the Buddha why he doesn't.

89
00:08:33,216 --> 00:08:42,811
And one of the reasons is that if he had said that he had a self or he didn't have a self, it would just make his confusion even greater.

90
00:08:43,753 --> 00:08:51,965
So here he's again talking to Vajragota and he's trying to explain what Nibbana is.

91
00:08:51,985 --> 00:08:53,508
So he says, what do you think, Vajra?

92
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:57,013
Suppose a fire was burning in front of you.

93
00:08:57,517 --> 00:08:59,980
Would you know this fire is burning in front of me?

94
00:09:00,581 --> 00:09:02,322
Yes, I would, worthy Gautama.

95
00:09:02,342 --> 00:09:09,152
But Vacha, supposing they were to ask you, this fire burning in front of you, what does it depend on to burn?

96
00:09:09,692 --> 00:09:10,514
How would you answer?

97
00:09:11,095 --> 00:09:12,135
I would answer like this.

98
00:09:12,777 --> 00:09:19,426
This fire is burning in front of me, burns on dependence, independence on grass and logs as fuel.

99
00:09:20,727 --> 00:09:24,131
Suppose that fire burning in front of you was extinguished.

100
00:09:25,039 --> 00:09:28,325
Would you know it was extinguished, that it was quenched?

101
00:09:28,764 --> 00:09:30,388
Yes, I would, worthy Gautama.

102
00:09:30,908 --> 00:09:38,919
But Vacha, supposing they were to ask you, this fire in front of you that is quenched, in what direction did it go?

103
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,062
East, south, west, north?

104
00:09:41,082 --> 00:09:42,024
How would you answer?

105
00:09:42,884 --> 00:09:45,149
It doesn't apply, worthy Gautama.

106
00:09:45,168 --> 00:09:48,312
The fire dependent on grass and logs as fuel...

107
00:09:48,697 --> 00:09:54,970
When that runs out and no more fuel is added, the fire is reckoned to have been quenched for lack of fuel.

108
00:09:55,650 --> 00:10:11,061
In the same way, Vacher, any form by which a realised one might be described has been given up, cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated and unable to rise in the future.

109
00:10:11,529 --> 00:10:22,280
Now, unfortunately, this has been often used as a way of saying that the Buddha is just talking about annihilation, complete extinction.

110
00:10:23,562 --> 00:10:37,596
But in fact, that doesn't take into consideration, which has only come out, I think, you know, fairly recently within the last 20 or so years, that what's not been taken into account is the science of the time.

111
00:10:38,658 --> 00:10:39,158
So,

112
00:10:39,879 --> 00:10:46,347
The fire element, like the earth element and the water element and the wind element are all around us.

113
00:10:46,427 --> 00:10:49,150
They're there as latent possibility.

114
00:10:50,212 --> 00:10:58,322
And given the right occasion, the element of fire will come down and grasp the object.

115
00:10:58,342 --> 00:11:02,707
So that's why it's a simile for grasping.

116
00:11:03,249 --> 00:11:08,335
So it's not as though the way we think of, you know,

117
00:11:09,057 --> 00:11:13,028
the log catching fire, meaning that there's something in the log that happens.

118
00:11:13,589 --> 00:11:20,807
You might say that the oxygen in the air sort of grasps the log, something like that.

119
00:11:21,109 --> 00:11:25,259
So we have this element of fire everywhere, the element of heat.

120
00:11:25,662 --> 00:11:31,711
Given certain circumstances, it will congregate around a particular object, a log.

121
00:11:32,231 --> 00:11:32,913
Right.

122
00:11:32,932 --> 00:11:35,316
And in so doing, it will consume it.

123
00:11:35,856 --> 00:11:40,283
Once it's consumed, it's not as though the fire is extinguished and it's gone completely.

124
00:11:40,423 --> 00:11:44,789
It goes back into its latent form and then it can't be described.

125
00:11:45,211 --> 00:11:45,490
Right.

126
00:11:45,510 --> 00:11:46,773
And you don't know where it is.

127
00:11:46,852 --> 00:11:48,936
It's just around you, you might say.

128
00:11:49,371 --> 00:12:01,235
And this is the way that the Buddha tries to explain that Nibbāna is simply beyond any conceiving, any imagination, anything like that.

129
00:12:01,355 --> 00:12:02,739
It's just beyond it.

130
00:12:08,089 --> 00:12:09,371
So the simile...

131
00:12:09,486 --> 00:12:14,192
Right, and now we come to another great simile, the simile of the house builder.

132
00:12:14,352 --> 00:12:18,217
And this, of course, is what we call his victory verses.

133
00:12:18,759 --> 00:12:22,663
They come from the Dhammapada, number 153 and 154.

134
00:12:24,105 --> 00:12:30,833
Oh, how many a birth in samsara have I wandered in vain, seeking the builder of this house?

135
00:12:31,835 --> 00:12:34,138
Repeated building is indeed suffering.

136
00:12:34,499 --> 00:12:36,961
Sorry, repeated birth is indeed suffering.

137
00:12:37,515 --> 00:12:39,197
Oh, house builder, you are seen.

138
00:12:39,238 --> 00:12:41,081
You will not build this house again.

139
00:12:41,140 --> 00:12:42,582
Your rafters are broken.

140
00:12:42,623 --> 00:12:44,144
Your ridge pole is shattered.

141
00:12:44,806 --> 00:12:46,969
My mind has reached the unconditioned.

142
00:12:47,389 --> 00:12:49,952
I have attained the destruction of craving.

143
00:12:51,575 --> 00:12:58,124
So this house builder, of course, is the self, right?

144
00:12:58,183 --> 00:12:59,725
There's this delusion of the self.

145
00:13:00,187 --> 00:13:02,028
And he's actually found the core of it.

146
00:13:02,769 --> 00:13:03,912
Now...

147
00:13:05,427 --> 00:13:08,330
Then there's another discourse where he uses the same thing.

148
00:13:08,470 --> 00:13:18,958
And this comes from a whole group of discourses known as the Opama Sutta or the Sutta on similes.

149
00:13:19,399 --> 00:13:23,943
And it's just devoted to discourses that are given with similes.

150
00:13:25,144 --> 00:13:28,888
And it's in the Sanyutanikā, the connected discourses.

151
00:13:31,671 --> 00:13:34,033
So this one, number one,

152
00:13:34,434 --> 00:13:35,437
It reads like this.

153
00:13:35,918 --> 00:13:41,652
So have I heard, at one time the Buddha was staying at Sabhati in Jeta's Grove in Atapindika's monastery.

154
00:13:42,553 --> 00:13:47,144
There the Buddha said, mendicants, the rafters of a bungalow, all lean to the peak.

155
00:13:47,716 --> 00:14:11,344
and meet at the peak and when the peak is demolished they're all demolished too in the same way all unskillful qualities are rooted in ignorance and meet in ignorance and when ignorance is demolished they're all demolished too so you should train yourself like this we will stay diligent that's how you should train yourself so here again we have the you know a very

156
00:14:11,846 --> 00:14:17,052
shall we say, obvious little simile about the houses they built.

157
00:14:18,934 --> 00:14:21,076
Most of them were just single stories, of course.

158
00:14:22,438 --> 00:14:26,322
There was a presumption that you'd be very rich if you had a two-story house.

159
00:14:27,384 --> 00:14:28,505
So they were all bungalows.

160
00:14:29,166 --> 00:14:34,392
And the ridge pole is the one right at the top of the roof.

161
00:14:34,773 --> 00:14:38,817
And if the roof collapses, that's real bad news for the house because

162
00:14:39,285 --> 00:14:42,331
You know, especially in the rainy season, you're going to be washed out.

163
00:14:43,173 --> 00:14:56,918
So here again, he uses this peak, this ridge pole to point to this essential delusion of ignorance, which is the self.

164
00:14:56,937 --> 00:15:03,690
And when that goes, everything that has been created by the self is also demolished.

165
00:15:04,192 --> 00:15:13,524
And you can't do anything which is unwholesome again, because the root cause of unwholesome is this delusion of being a self.

166
00:15:13,544 --> 00:15:14,546
What do we mean again?

167
00:15:15,027 --> 00:15:18,510
Reminding ourselves, what do we mean by being a self?

168
00:15:18,812 --> 00:15:25,941
We mean that we think, we believe ourselves, we experience ourselves to be this human being as such.

169
00:15:26,621 --> 00:15:31,567
And this human being is seeking happiness, but it does so through the sensual pleasures of

170
00:15:32,105 --> 00:15:36,572
that the body offers and the mind offers, the heart offers, and that's the problem.

171
00:15:37,634 --> 00:15:45,187
So when the self goes, all the unwholesome stuff that's dependent on that also disappears.

172
00:15:45,888 --> 00:15:47,149
But you still keep living.

173
00:15:47,169 --> 00:15:54,461
The Buddha said the only thing that goes is this essential delusion, greed, acquisitiveness, and aversion.

174
00:15:54,682 --> 00:15:57,386
Aversion meaning both aversion and fear.

175
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:05,609
And as a final one, the preciousness of human birth.

176
00:16:05,629 --> 00:16:09,313
So this again is number two within this collection of similes.

177
00:16:10,937 --> 00:16:15,783
Then the Buddha, picking up a little dirt in his fingernail, addressed the mendicants.

178
00:16:15,802 --> 00:16:16,904
What do you think, mendicants?

179
00:16:17,445 --> 00:16:21,770
Which is more, this little bit of dirt on my fingernail or this great earth?

180
00:16:22,291 --> 00:16:29,019
Sir, the Great Earth is far more, and a little bit of dirt on your fingernail is tiny compared to the Great Earth.

181
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:30,241
It doesn't count.

182
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:31,303
There's no comparison.

183
00:16:31,783 --> 00:16:32,806
It's not worth a fraction.

184
00:16:33,567 --> 00:16:40,897
In the same way, the sentient beings reborn as humans are few, while those not reborn as humans are many.

185
00:16:41,177 --> 00:16:42,759
So you should train yourself thus.

186
00:16:43,660 --> 00:16:44,863
We will remain diligent.

187
00:16:46,325 --> 00:16:47,927
That's how you should train yourself.

188
00:16:49,476 --> 00:16:56,631
And this is supported by another very famous one about the turtle, the blind turtle.

189
00:16:58,195 --> 00:16:58,294
Yeah.

190
00:16:58,315 --> 00:17:02,123
And this comes from also the connected sayings.

191
00:17:02,143 --> 00:17:09,357
Monks, suppose this great earth were totally covered with water and a man were to toss a yoke with a single hole there.

192
00:17:10,553 --> 00:17:12,836
A wind from the east would push it to the west.

193
00:17:12,955 --> 00:17:15,058
A wind from the west push it to the east.

194
00:17:15,618 --> 00:17:17,642
A wind from the north push it to the south.

195
00:17:17,701 --> 00:17:19,523
A wind from the south push it to the north.

196
00:17:19,785 --> 00:17:22,087
And suppose a blind turtle were there.

197
00:17:22,828 --> 00:17:25,751
It would come to the surface once every hundred years.

198
00:17:25,873 --> 00:17:26,613
What do you think?

199
00:17:27,074 --> 00:17:34,923
Would that blind turtle coming to the surface once every hundred years stick his neck into the yoke with a single hole?

200
00:17:35,748 --> 00:17:47,000
Oh, it would be sheer coincidence, Lord, that the blind sea turtle coming to the surface once every hundred years would stick his neck into the yoke with a single hole.

201
00:17:48,163 --> 00:17:52,307
It's likewise a sheer coincidence that one obtains the human state.

202
00:17:53,028 --> 00:18:00,237
It's likewise sheer coincidence that a Tathagata, worthy and rightfully self-awakened, arises in the world.

203
00:18:00,857 --> 00:18:07,771
It is likewise sheer coincidence that the Dhamma and the Vinaya expounded by the Tathagata appears in the world.

204
00:18:08,653 --> 00:18:11,679
Now this human state has been attained.

205
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:16,087
A Tathagata worthy and rightly self-awakened has arisen in the world.

206
00:18:16,588 --> 00:18:21,458
A Dhamma and a Vinaya expounded by the Tathagata appears in the world.

207
00:18:21,578 --> 00:18:22,359
Therefore,

208
00:18:22,660 --> 00:18:26,588
Your duty is to contemplate this is suffering.

209
00:18:27,089 --> 00:18:29,034
This is the origin of suffering.

210
00:18:29,315 --> 00:18:31,378
This is the cessation of suffering.

211
00:18:31,599 --> 00:18:39,959
Your duty is to contemplate this is the path of practice leading to the cessation of suffering.

212
00:18:39,979 --> 00:18:42,423
So here we have this idea of

213
00:18:42,656 --> 00:18:57,527
coincidence and we can begin to understand that when we realize that every moment is this meeting of all of our past causes with present conditions.

214
00:18:57,962 --> 00:18:58,743
Oops, just a minute.

215
00:18:58,763 --> 00:19:00,346
We are with present conditions.

216
00:19:00,386 --> 00:19:02,931
So these past causes are coming.

217
00:19:03,050 --> 00:19:04,534
They hit the present conditions.

218
00:19:04,834 --> 00:19:08,901
So we never know exactly what's going to happen moment after moment.

219
00:19:09,461 --> 00:19:16,913
I mean, when we live like this in a country which is fairly peaceful, you know, we can normally predict that you're going to wake up tomorrow.

220
00:19:16,933 --> 00:19:22,923
But but not if you're in Ukraine or if you're in Lebanon or even Gaza.

221
00:19:23,326 --> 00:19:29,056
And so there's a sense of unknowability, of sheer coincidence.

222
00:19:29,616 --> 00:19:38,532
And this is how he gets across to us the sheer fortunate birth, you know, being born as a human being.

223
00:19:38,851 --> 00:19:44,218
And why is it that as human beings, this is the best place to be to become liberated?

224
00:19:44,657 --> 00:19:55,811
Because there is this joy and woe, and there is this awakenedness, this intuitive awareness developed enough to investigate.

225
00:19:56,413 --> 00:20:04,182
And it's that process of investigation into the three characteristics of existence, which is the process of liberation.

226
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:06,282
There we are.

227
00:20:06,683 --> 00:20:18,357
So I can only hope my words have been of some assistance and that by, in the words of the Buddha, through our diligence, we will become fully liberated sooner rather than later.

